OCI (Open Container Initiative): a standard

on Mac:
brew install podman
//containers are a Linux technology, the only way to run containers on other platforms is to rely on a Limux virtual machine, podman on MacOS and Windows requires a virtual machine. 
//the default machine name os podman-machine-default if a machine name is not specified as an argument, then 'podman-machine-default' will be started.
//only one podman virtual machine can be started, if a virtual machine is already running, podman machine start returns an error.
podman machine init
podman machine start    
podman info
podman version


default registry location:
/etc/containers/registries.conf

customise a registry config file:
$ mkdir .config/containers
$ vim .config/containers/registries.conf
//inside of the file
unqualified-search-registries = ['docker.io', 'ghrc.io', 'quay.io']

some useful commands: 
$ podman search <image-name>        //search the image in registries
eg. $podman search busybox
$ podman pull <image-name>
$ podman images                     //verify

$ podman run -it <image-name>       //create and run a new container for an image, -it tells podman to allocate a virtual terminal session within the container
best practice: $ podman run -it --rm <image-name>             //avoid filling up file system
$ podman ps                         //shows running containers
$ podman ps -a

when you run $ podman run -it <image-name>, you are actually inside the container, so you can
/ # ls
/ # exit    //stop running the container, or ctrl+C instead

$ podman run --name <container-name> \ -p ext_port:int_port <container-IMAGE>
$ podman start <container-name>
$ podman inspect <container-name>
$ podman port <container-name>
$ podman stop <container-name>
$ pdoman rm <container-name>
$ pdoman rmi <container-IMAGE>

eg.
$ podman run --name pdm-nginx -p 8080:80 nginx        //this command pull nginx image if it's not present and runs a new cotnainer named 'pdm-nginx'; -p assigns port 80 in the container to host port 8080 on my machine
$ exit
$ podman start pdm-nginx                              //start running the container again in detach mode
$ podman inspect pdm-nginx                            //get more information of the container in json format
$ odman port pdm-nginx                                //check the port that container is using
$ curl localhost:8080
$ podman stop pdm-nginx                               //stop running a cotnainer on the host machine, 'exit' is used inside a container's shell
$ podman rm pdm-nginx                                 //remove container
$ podman rmi <image-ID>                               //remove image, image ID can be retrieved by running $ podman images


workflow: building a cotnainer image
$ vim Dockerfile
$ podman build -t <image-name> .
$ podman run --name <container-name> \ -p 8080:8080 <image-name>:<tag>
